  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nVariable cloudiness is occurring overnight ahead of an\napproaching cold front from the west. This front will begin to\ncross the region from west to east just prior to daybreak and\nbe to the east of the region later this morning. A couple of \nrounds of showers are expected, one along the front and another \nin its wake with areas north and west of the Capital District \nthe most likely areas seeing these showers. However, an \nisolated shower cannot be ruled out within the Capital District \neither. West to northwest winds will pick up behind the front\nwith frequent gusts of 25 to 35 mph expected with some locally\nhigher gusts possible. The strongest winds will likely be\nlocated within the Mohawk Valley, Capital District and\nBerkshires. High temperatures will reach the 50s and 60s today\nwith slowly falling temperatures this afternoon as cold air\nadvection continues at the surface and aloft.\n\n850 hPa temperatures fall to near or slightly below 0C tonight.\nWinds will lessen a little tonight. Temperatures fall back to \nthe 30s to lower 40s except some upper 20s across the \nAdirondacks. Temperatures and winds look to remain elevated \nenough to prevent frost formation within the Upper Hudson Valley\ntonight.\n\nHigh pressure continues to build into the region on Tuesday\nwhich will allow for continued breezy conditions but gusts will\nbe less than on Monday on the order of 15 to 25 mph. Tuesday\nwill be a cool day with highs only in the 50s for most except\nlower 60s across the mid-Hudson Valley and upper 40s across\nportions of the Adirondacks and southern Greens. Winds will\nbecome light to calm Tuesday night with the high cresting\noverhead as lows quickly fall into the 30s for most. Patchy \nfrost is more likely in the Upper Hudson Valley Tuesday night \nwhere frost advisories may be needed later in time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSouth to southwesterly return flow returns Wednesday into the \nlate week period as the high departs to the east. Temperatures \nwill rebound back closer to normal values. A longwave trough \nsets up over the region later this week with most of the forcing\nfocused along the US/Canada border. A few disturbances look to \npass across southern Canada but close enough to bring a few \nlight rain showers to northern and western areas at times. \nHowever, no significant precipitation is expected during this \nperiod. Dry weather looks likely for next weekend with high \npressure overhead.",
